(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-ii-quiz"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies II Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which is a poor prognostic factor in non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","choices":["B-cell rather than T-cell type","Elevated lactate dehydrogenase","Age under 60","Stage I disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Poor prognostic factors are age over 60, extranodal sites, poor performance status (an Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group grade above 2), stage III or IV disease and an elevated lactate dehydrogenase. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What proportion of Hodgkin lymphoma patients are alive at 5 years?","choices":["About 50%","About 60%","About 78%","Over 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is generally curable, with more than 90% of patients alive at 5 years. Relapse after first-line treatment may require allogeneic stem cell transplant. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 30, 42, 54"},{"q":"Which symptoms reflect the hypercalcemia of multiple myeloma?","choices":["Constipation and thirst","Generalized swelling","Fatigue and breathlessness","Back and pelvic pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the CRAB (calcium, renal insufficiency, anemia, bone lesions) features, hypercalcemia causes constipation, increased thirst, urinary frequency and nausea. Renal insufficiency causes swelling and anemia causes fatigue and shortness of breath. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Splenic marginal zone lymphoma is associated with which infection?","choices":["Helicobacter pylori","Human immunodeficiency virus","Human herpesvirus 8","Hepatitis C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Splenic marginal zone lymphoma is associated with hepatitis C. Marginal zone lymphomas make up 5-17% of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, are more common in older adults, and the gastric extranodal form is tied to H. pylori. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What are Reed-Sternberg cells?","choices":["Binucleated, neoplastic B cells","Benign clearing macrophages","Clonal plasma cells","Neoplastic T cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of B-cell lymphoma characterized by few malignant cells, the Reed-Sternberg cells, which are binucleated, neoplastic B cells, set among numerous immune effector cells. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 36, 32, 44"},{"q":"At what size is a persistent, enlarging lymph node considered significant enough for biopsy?","choices":["0.5 cm","1 cm","2 cm","5 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Excisional biopsy is reserved for significant enlargement (2 cm), persistent, and progressively increasing in size. The whole node is removed so its architecture can be seen. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the approximate yearly risk of MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) progressing to myeloma?","choices":["About 1% per year","About 10% per year","About 25% per year","About 50% per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) carries a risk of myeloma progression of about 1% per year. Smoldering myeloma progresses faster, about 10% per year. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How is early, non-bulky stage I or II Hodgkin lymphoma without B symptoms treated?","choices":["Watch and wait","Allogeneic stem cell transplant","A short course of chemotherapy","Chemotherapy plus radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early stage I and stage II Hodgkin lymphoma without bulk or B symptoms is treated with a short course of chemotherapy, historically ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine). Bulky disease or B symptoms add radiation. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How much is Hodgkin lymphoma risk raised in a same-sex sibling of a patient?","choices":["2 times","4 times","10 times","50 times"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Same-sex siblings of a patient have a 10 times increased risk of also developing Hodgkin lymphoma. Epstein-Barr virus is a suspected risk factor, found in 40-50% of cases. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 6, 37"},{"q":"How is asymptomatic chronic lymphocytic leukemia without rapid progression managed?","choices":["Immediate targeted therapy","Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Single-node radiation","Observation (watch and wait)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic patients may be observed, watch and wait, until there is evidence of rapid progression (a lymphocyte count doubling in under 6 months) or disease-related symptoms. Treatment begins with symptomatic disease or rapid progression. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 27, 42"},{"q":"Which statement belongs in education for a patient newly diagnosed with chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["Admission is needed to start therapy","No follow-up is needed once stable","The long-term outlook is poor","Hospital stays are unlikely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients are unlikely to require hospitalization, and treatment is likely based on patient preferences and mutations. They will need follow-up for lab monitoring and treatment, but the long-term prognosis is likely very good. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 13, 34"},{"q":"Under the R-ISS (Revised International Staging System), a myeloma patient who meets neither stage I nor stage III criteria is which stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the R-ISS (Revised International Staging System), stage II is defined as not stage I or III. Stage I needs low beta-2 microglobulin, normal albumin, standard-risk chromosomes and normal lactate dehydrogenase; stage III needs high beta-2 microglobulin with high-risk chromosomes and high lactate dehydrogenase. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the main focus of treatment in cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Rituximab-based chemotherapy","The skin manifestations","Visceral organ disease","Antifungal therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatments for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides) focus on the cutaneous manifestations, because there is often no visceral involvement. The aggressive Sezary form brings severe itching that lowers quality of life. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 22, 27"},{"q":"Which T-cell lymphoma is associated with human T-lymphotropic virus 1?","choices":["Mycosis fungoides","Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma","Anaplastic large cell lymphoma","Hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma is very aggressive, associated with human T-lymphotropic virus 1, and more common in the Caribbean, Central America and South America. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the approximate 5-year relative survival of multiple myeloma?","choices":["About 30%","About 60%","About 88%","About 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The 5-year relative survival of multiple myeloma is approximately 60% (data from 2018-2022). Each line of therapy brings shorter remissions, though newer therapies may extend survival past 10 years. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 54, 12, 42"},{"q":"About what share of non-Hodgkin lymphomas are B-cell lymphomas?","choices":["4%","15%","50%","85%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 85% of non-Hodgkin lymphomas are B-cell and 15% are T-cell or natural killer-cell. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a diverse group of lymphoid malignancies that present frequently with lymphadenopathy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Most multiple myeloma diagnoses occur above which age?","choices":["35 years","45 years","65 years","85 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Myeloma risk increases with age, and most diagnoses are made over age 65. It is a disease of older adults, also more common in African Americans and in those with prior monoclonal gammopathy or smoldering myeloma. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the goal of treatment in multiple myeloma?","choices":["Observation until symptoms appear","Complete bone marrow eradication","Long remissions and quality of life","Cure after a single regimen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because myeloma is by definition symptomatic and considered incurable, the goal of treatment is maintaining prolonged remissions to improve survival while maintaining quality of life. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 49, 52, 54"},{"q":"Which regimen is an example of multi-drug therapy for aggressive B-cell lymphoma?","choices":["CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone) without rituximab","Venetoclax plus obinutuzumab for a fixed duration","Rituximab plus CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone)","ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine) without rituximab"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aggressive B-cell lymphomas are often treated with multi-drug chemotherapy such as R-CHOP (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, doxorubicin, prednisone). Rituximab is the anti-cluster of differentiation 20 monoclonal antibody. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 11, 41"},{"q":"How is chronic lymphocytic leukemia most often first discovered?","choices":["From a fast-growing mass","On routine blood work","From B symptoms","From a bleeding episode"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is frequently diagnosed while the patient is asymptomatic, on routine blood work that shows a lymphocytosis. Symptoms, when present, come from nodes, cytopenias or B symptoms. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 7, 31"},{"q":"In the lymphoma work-up, what does an elevated lactate dehydrogenase reflect?","choices":["Rapid proliferation","Marrow involvement","Hyperviscosity","Paraprotein level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The work-up includes LDH (lactate dehydrogenase), which reflects rapid proliferation, alongside a complete blood count for cytopenias from marrow involvement and chemistries to rule out tumor lysis syndrome. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 25, 18, 50"},{"q":"How is a single-node, non-aggressive non-Hodgkin lymphoma treated?","choices":["Radiation","Multi-drug chemotherapy","Autologous transplant","Allopurinol and fluids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management depends heavily on subtype and presentation; a single-node, non-aggressive lymphoma is treated with radiation. Aggressive B-cell lymphomas instead get multi-drug chemotherapy with rituximab. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which group has a higher frequency of chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["People of Caribbean descent","African American people","People from sub-Saharan Africa","People of Jewish descent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is more common in Eastern Europeans and in people of Jewish descent, and twice as common in males. No occupational or environmental risk factor is known. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 6, 21, 32, 46"},{"q":"Which test confirms the diagnosis of chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["Excisional node biopsy","Protein electrophoresis","Flow cytometry","Bone marrow biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed by flow cytometry, which identifies the clonal B-cell population, coexpressing the cluster of differentiation markers 19 and 5. A bone marrow biopsy is usually not required. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which system is used to stage lymphoma?","choices":["Lugano classification","Rai classification","Revised International Staging System","Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoma staging is based on the Lugano classification, a modified version of the older Ann Arbor system, which stages by the involved node regions and organs relative to the diaphragm. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 10, 26, 30, 51"},{"q":"On starting treatment for non-Hodgkin lymphoma, what should a patient be told to do?","choices":["Take potassium supplements","Stop allopurinol during treatment","Drink fluids and take allopurinol","Limit fluids to protect the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Upon starting treatment, patients should drink plenty of fluids and take allopurinol (a hypouricemic agent), given in advance because tumor lysis syndrome should be anticipated. Care is managed by a hematologist, ideally a lymphoma expert. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 28, 34"},{"q":"In the Rai classification, lymphocytosis with lymphadenopathy, and no organomegaly or cytopenia, is which stage?","choices":["Stage 0","Stage I","Stage II","Stage III"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rai stage I is lymphocytosis and lymphadenopathy. Stage 0 is asymptomatic lymphocytosis alone, stage II adds organomegaly (hepatomegaly and/or splenomegaly), stage III is lymphocytosis with anemia and stage IV is lymphocytosis with thrombocytopenia. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Sporadic Burkitt lymphoma is almost always seen below which age?","choices":["20 years","35 years","50 years","60 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sporadic Burkitt lymphoma is typically seen in the United States and Western Europe, more in males, and almost always below age 35. That makes it a disease of children and young adults. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What referral does suspected Burkitt lymphoma require?","choices":["Routine hematology referral","Surgical debulking first","Watchful waiting with recheck","Emergent hematology referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment must start promptly with emergent referral to a hematologist, because the doubling time is extremely fast and tumor size can expand within the same 24 hours. Burkitt lymphoma requires rapid transfer of care. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 33, 34"},{"q":"What role do cytogenetics play in chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["Prognosis and treatment","Confirming the diagnosis","Assigning the Rai stage","Ruling out a reactive cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cytogenetics are important for prognosis and carry some treatment considerations. They do not confirm the diagnosis, which comes from flow cytometry, and they are not needed for Rai staging, which uses exam and labs. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 10"}]};
